## Lore

The hawrk-ohl, often called Owl Men, are powerful bird-like predators that look like humanoid owls. They come from the same world as the zembahk and are cousins of the other hawrks, preying on the zembahk, the hawrk-duhk and other smaller creatures. Their great strength and their complete imperviousness to magic, a defense evolved on a world ruled by mystic energy, are what the Splugorth advertise when they sell them as hunters, guards, protectors and laborers.

They are aggressive predators that will attack prey of man size and larger, not very intelligent and driven by instinct and self preservation. Unlike the hawrk-ka they do not love killing, they are simply very good at it, fearless and merciless in combat, though they avoid fighting to the death when they can. They enjoy brawls and contests of strength and skill. They are far more social than the hawrk-ka, living in flock-tribes of four to 24 (never more than 60) that rarely fight among themselves and share territory, prey and possessions. They will mix with other humanoids but hate the hawrk-ka and hawrk-duhk and avoid them except to fight. At home they use swords and maces, but they happily learn energy weapons; heavy weapons, magic weapons, bows and vibro-blades are favorites. They love to attack by leaping from trees and high places.

A male mates for life with one female, once every three years; she lays 1D4 eggs that hatch in four months, and the young stay with the parents 1D4 years though they mature in six months. About a quarter stay with the flock-tribe. Males and females are about equal in number, equally aggressive and about the same size, the females less colorful.

Alignment: any, but generally anarchist or evil. Weight 700 lbs (315 kg); typically 10 to 14 ft (3 to 4.3 m) tall. Typical NPCs are level 1D4+1. Average life span fifty years. Carnivores that may prey on humanoids and hawrk-duhk as well as animals.

## GM Notes

Slave market value 250,000 to 400,000 credits. Sold in Atlantis and occasionally found in North America, Europe and Africa. Enemies: larger predators, the supernatural and humanoids. Work well with groups but are always suspicious of others, especially strangers and men of magic.
